A simple gold signet ring on a dark velvet cloth. The ring is old but well-crafted — not flashy, not magical, just a plain noble's ring. A flat oval bezel with a worn coat of arms barely visible (too eroded to read). The gold is slightly dull, with tiny scratches from centuries of use. No gemstones, no glow, no enchantment — this is a mundane object whose importance is purely political.

The ring is small — sized for a slender hand. It sits alone on the cloth, slightly tilted, catching warm candlelight from one side. Soft shadows. The velvet has a few dust motes.

STYLE: Realistic still life, painterly, warm tones (gold, deep burgundy velvet, amber light). Think Dutch Golden Age painting — intimate, quiet, focused on a single object. Dark background fading to black.

MOOD: Understated importance. This ring doesn't look special — and that's the point. It could be any noble's ring. But someone wants it very badly.
